---
/**
 * ============================================================================
 * MORE ON ME SECTION (MoreOnMe.astro)
 * ============================================================================
 * What this component does:
 * - Features interactive expandable cards showcasing your core qualities.
 * - Displays a 1-second hover preview and locks open/closed when clicked.
 * - Powered by TypeScript in src/scripts/traits.ts.
 *
 * HOW TO MAINTAIN:
 * - To edit trait titles: update the <h3 class="trait-title"> in each card.
 * - To edit trait stories: update the paragraphs inside <div class="trait-inner">.
 * ============================================================================
 */
---

<section id="more-on-me" class="story-section container">
  <h2>More On Me</h2>

  <div class="traits-list">
    <!-- TRAIT 1: Creative Problem Solving (Left-aligned) -->
    <article class="trait-card trait-card-left" data-trait="1">
      <div class="trait-label-side">
        <h3 class="trait-title">My Strongest Quality: Creative Problem Solving</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="trait-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="trait-drawer" id="trait-drawer-1">
        <div class="trait-inner">
          <p>
            Creative problem solving is where I bring the most value. I approach
            problems logically, but I stay flexible when the first path is not
            the best one. Whether I am supporting users with unexpected requests
            or designing a technical solution, I focus on understanding the core
            need and building a response that is both effective and thoughtful.
          </p>
          <p>
            That mindset was sharpened while teaching students with different
            learning needs and while working in a busy New York City store where
            not every customer request had a standard answer. In both settings, I
            learned how to stay calm, ask better questions, and create solutions
            that feel tailored instead of generic.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <button class="trait-toggle-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="trait-drawer-1" aria-label="Toggle My Strongest Quality: Creative Problem Solving">
        <span class="trait-indicator" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="trait-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22">
            <path d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </svg>
        </span>
      </button>
    </article>

    <!-- TRAIT 2: Communication That Builds Trust (Right-aligned) -->
    <article class="trait-card trait-card-right" data-trait="2">
      <button class="trait-toggle-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="trait-drawer-2" aria-label="Toggle Communication That Builds Trust">
        <span class="trait-indicator" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="trait-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22">
            <path d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </svg>
        </span>
      </button>
      <div class="trait-drawer" id="trait-drawer-2">
        <div class="trait-inner">
          <p>
            I am an effective communicator because I have spent years translating
            ideas across different levels of experience. I started volunteering as
            a teacher at 14, and that work has made me patient, clear, and
            genuinely attentive to what others are trying to say.
          </p>
          <p>
            In technical environments, this means I can break down complex ideas,
            align with teammates, and keep collaboration productive. In practical
            terms, I do not just explain my point; I make sure the other person is
            understood too. That two-way communication helps projects move faster
            with fewer misunderstandings.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="trait-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="trait-label-side">
        <h3 class="trait-title">Communication That Builds Trust</h3>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- TRAIT 3: Fast Learning with Follow-Through (Left-aligned) -->
    <article class="trait-card trait-card-left" data-trait="3">
      <div class="trait-label-side">
        <h3 class="trait-title">Fast Learning with Follow-Through</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="trait-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="trait-drawer" id="trait-drawer-3">
        <div class="trait-inner">
          <p>
            I learn quickly, especially under pressure. In my cryptography course,
            I had to master advanced topics beyond my previous background,
            including modular arithmetic and field theory, within a short period.
          </p>
          <p>
            I treated that challenge like an engineering problem: break it down,
            study consistently, test my understanding, and improve until I reached
            mastery. That same approach carries into software work whenever I need
            to adopt new tools, frameworks, or domains on a tight timeline.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <button class="trait-toggle-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="trait-drawer-3" aria-label="Toggle Fast Learning with Follow-Through">
        <span class="trait-indicator" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="trait-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22">
            <path d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </svg>
        </span>
      </button>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Load TypeScript trait expansion behavior -->
<script src="../scripts/traits.ts"></script>
